tamework.ui.guide.topic.gettingStarted.title=Getting started
tamework.ui.guide.topic.gettingStarted.body=TAME, OWN, LINK\nTaming unlocks supported companion interactions. Ownership decides who may use them. Linking adds an eligible companion to a command tool; it does not tame an animal or give you ownership.\n\nYOUR FIRST COMPANION\nRead the creature's prompt and use the requested item or interaction. Once it is yours, use a compatible command item to link it, open the command panel, and choose Follow. Available tools and how to obtain them come from your animal pack.\n\nINTERACTIONS AND NAMES\nPrompts change with your held item, crouching, ownership, and the animal's condition. Naming items usually require a tamed animal you own; their input window explains any name restrictions.\n\nWHEN NOTHING HAPPENS\nCheck ownership, tame status, held item, cooldowns, and the current prompt. Not every creature supports every system. Use Reading a card to inspect its state, then Commands & groups to choose which animals receive commands.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.gettingStarted.visual=Wild → tamed → linked
tamework.ui.guide.topic.gettingStarted.note=1 Tame using the creature's prompt.  2 Confirm it belongs to you.  3 Link with a compatible tool. Linking and ownership are separate.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.readingCard.title=Reading a card
tamework.ui.guide.topic.readingCard.body=IDENTITY AND STATUS\nThe header identifies your companion. Its portrait is a static appearance image, not a live preview. Species, life stage, health, traits, level, talent points, needs, and cooldowns appear where supported. Hover over icons and meters for details.\n\nWHAT EACH AREA MEANS\n1 Identity: name, appearance, and current or saved state.\n2 Progression: trait effects, level, and available talents.\n3 Care: happiness, hunger, thirst, and relevant timers.\n4 Actions: controls available for this companion's state.\n\nCHOOSE YOUR VIEW\nLinked shows this tool's links. Nearby shows eligible animals around you. Owned includes your animals off-screen and in other worlds without requiring a tool link. Bonded tools have their own roster filters.\n\nSORT AND FILTER\nUse name, species, or group filters where available. Care sorts put the lowest percentages first; unknown values appear last. Inactive animals can remain visible. Off-screen information is saved or last known, so it may not describe the animal's current surroundings.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.readingCard.visual=Know your companion
tamework.ui.guide.topic.readingCard.note=The gold numbers identify the card areas described above. Example values explain the UI; enabled features and real values vary.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.commandsGroups.title=Commands & groups
tamework.ui.guide.topic.commandsGroups.body=ASSIGN AND USE\nOpen the command menu with your tool's secondary input. Choose commands for the displayed input slots. Changing an assignment keeps the menu open and does not issue that command. Use the assigned input with the tool to act.\n\nCOMMON COMMANDS\nFollow travels with you; Hold stays in place; Idle returns to the animal's idle behavior. Defend and Aggressive select supported combat behavior. Attack Target directs an attack; Move To Ping sends animals to the chosen position. Exact behavior and available commands depend on the pack.\n\nACTIVE ANIMALS AND GROUPS\nBulk commands use active companions. Inactive animals stay linked for individual management. Create, rename, recolor, or delete groups through the group controls, and assign animals with their card's group selector. Group shortcuts can activate one group while deactivating the rest, or set all active/inactive.\n\nHIGHLIGHT AND TROUBLESHOOT\nHighlight Active marks eligible loaded companions while you hold the tool. Groups organize commands, not ownership or storage. If a command fails, check active selection, links, ownership, state, and travel rules. See Travel & flight for home and recall.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.commandsGroups.visual=Only the active group receives bulk commands
tamework.ui.guide.topic.commandsGroups.note=Meadow is active in this example; Barn is inactive. A companion can stay linked while excluded from bulk commands.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.careHappiness.title=Care & happiness
tamework.ui.guide.topic.careHappiness.body=FOOD AND WATER\nHunger and thirst show remaining needs. Refill them through supported feeding, drinking, or trough interactions. Troughs may need food or water refills. Accepted resources depend on the animal pack.\n\nHAPPINESS\nCare and other configured factors affect happiness. Where available, hover for the current value, target trend, and temporary modifiers such as recent feeding, petting, or damage. A full need meter alone does not guarantee maximum happiness.\n\nBREEDING MARK\nThe thin red mark on happiness is the configured breeding requirement. Reaching it passes that happiness check; partner, maturity, cooldown, and other breeding rules still apply. It is absent when no requirement applies.\n\nCRITICAL NEEDS AND HARVESTS\nPoor needs may reduce happiness or block behaviors. Some servers enable damage after needs remain critical. With needs disabled, happiness treats needs as full without refilling saved hunger or thirst.\nSupported animals also show harvest readiness or a remaining cooldown. Read the animal's prompt to harvest.\n\nSee Breeding & growth for readiness and Capture & coops for storage.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.careHappiness.visual=Compare low needs with improved care
tamework.ui.guide.topic.careHappiness.note=3 Care meters: higher food and water values can improve care. The red happiness tick marks a breeding requirement, not guaranteed readiness.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.breedingGrowth.title=Breeding & growth
tamework.ui.guide.topic.breedingGrowth.body=PREPARE THE PARENTS\nUse the breeding toggle where available. Keep compatible parents nearby, and meet the species' maturity, tame, wakefulness, and happiness rules. Hover over the breeding toggle for its happiness requirement.\n\nREADY OR WAITING\nA remaining timer means a cooldown is still running. A ready animal still needs an eligible partner and must pass local rules. Low happiness, a disabled toggle, claim rules, or a nearby population cap may block breeding.\n\nPOPULATION AND LITTERS\nNearby creatures and pending offspring count toward the configured local cap, so a litter may be reduced or refused. Fertility can produce zero through four offspring. Several similar siblings are not necessarily duplicates.\n\nGROWING UP\nOffspring may begin as babies or juveniles and grow into adults on the species' schedule. Life stage can restrict interactions. Inherited traits can influence the offspring's stats and behavior.\n\nUse Care & happiness to understand the happiness mark and Traits & talents to inspect inherited qualities. Example thresholds here are illustrative, not universal breeding requirements.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.breedingGrowth.visual=Two eligible parents → offspring
tamework.ui.guide.topic.breedingGrowth.note=Parent readiness is only part of the check. A compatible partner, local space, and the species' rules must also allow breeding.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.traitsTalents.title=Traits & talents
tamework.ui.guide.topic.traitsTalents.body=TRAITS AND TALENTS ARE DIFFERENT\nTraits are rolled or inherited qualities. Hover over actual trait icons to inspect effects such as health, strength, toughness, swiftness, fertility, size, disposition, or bounty where supported.\nTalents are choices purchased in a companion's configured talent tree.\n\nLEVELS AND POINTS\nSupported companions earn XP from their configured activities. Level and XP show progression; available talent points indicate choices you can spend. Open Talents on the companion's card, inspect prerequisites and costs, and choose an available node. Use reset only when offered and review its conditions.\n\nSAVED COMPANIONS\nDead and LOST companions can have talents edited when a complete saved restoration snapshot is available; those changes carry through revival or recovery. Owned mode does not require an item link for this. Ordinary unloaded animals must load before you can spend points. Older incomplete snapshots cannot support offline spending.\n\nBonded companions show live progression while active and saved progression while stored or dead. The animal pack controls XP sources, trait pools, talent effects, and reset options.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.traitsTalents.visual=Inspect traits, then choose talents
tamework.ui.guide.topic.traitsTalents.note=2 Traits describe qualities; talent points purchase choices. Check prerequisites on the real talent tree before spending.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.captureCoops.title=Capture & coops
tamework.ui.guide.topic.captureCoops.body=CAPTURE AND RELEASE\nUse a compatible empty capture item on an eligible living animal. The filled item holds that companion until released. Capture preserves current health; it does not heal the animal, and dead or zero-health animals cannot be captured.\n\nWHAT IS KEPT\nSupported storage retains the companion's identity, name, appearance, and progression. Ownership may be cleared or reassigned if the pack explicitly configures it. Moving between live and stored states does not create a second companion.\n\nCOOPS\nConfigured coops can house eligible animals and release them later. Some support placing a filled capture item directly into an available slot; other items use their normal release interaction. Follow the coop's prompts.\n\nREAD THE STATUS\nCaptured means use the filled item's release interaction. In Coop means release through that coop. Recall and Return Home do not replace these release actions, and stored animals cannot receive normal live commands.\n\nSee Finding & recovery for last-seen item and container locations. Trough care is covered in Care & happiness.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.captureCoops.visual=Live → captured or housed → released
tamework.ui.guide.topic.captureCoops.note=The same companion keeps its name and saved progression through supported storage. Stored health is not a free heal.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.findingRecovery.title=Finding & recovery
tamework.ui.guide.topic.findingRecovery.body=READ THE SAVED STATE\nLoaded: available in the world. Unloaded: off-screen with saved information. Captured: held in a filled item. In Coop: housed in a coop. Dead: a confirmed death. LOST: a saved restorable state after confirmed destructive removal, not merely an absent animal.\n\nFIND OR RECALL\nLocate can show a position, carrier, container, or last-seen item sighting. Last seen may be stale. Unknown means no usable sighting, not proof of death or destruction. Release captured items and coop occupants through their storage.\nRecall may retry an unloaded relocation. Expiry stops that attempt; it does not make the animal LOST.\n\nREVIVE OR RECOVER\nUse the offered restoration action for Dead or LOST companions after any cooldown. It requires supported saved state and policy. Review exact item costs when a confirmation appears.\n\nREMOVAL IS DIFFERENT\nUnlink removes a tool link but keeps ownership. Release permanently clears ownership and frees its slot; captured/cooped animals must leave storage first. Cull kills an eligible loaded living animal. Read the named confirmation before a permanent action.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.findingRecovery.visual=Match the status to the next action
tamework.ui.guide.topic.findingRecovery.note=Unloaded → Locate/Recall when allowed. Captured/In Coop → release from storage. Dead/LOST → inspect the offered restoration action and costs.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.bondedCompanions.title=Bonded companions
tamework.ui.guide.topic.bondedCompanions.body=YOUR ROSTER\nBonded companions use a roster rather than ordinary animal links. All, Active, Stored, and Dead filters help you find them; name search and sorting organize the list.\n\nSUMMON AND DISMISS\nSummon brings an eligible stored companion into the world. Dismiss returns an active companion to storage. Active-capacity limits can restrict summons; hover over the capacity label when multiple limits apply. Finite summon sessions and cooldowns show remaining time.\n\nPROGRESSION AND HEALTH\nActive cards show current health, level, XP, and available talent points. Stored and dead cards show saved progression; stored health is muted and dead health is empty. These cards omit ordinary happiness, hunger, and thirst meters.\n\nREVIVE AND REMOVE\nDead companions may offer Revive after policy and cooldown checks. Review any exact item costs. The card's removal control opens permanent deletion confirmation; Cancel keeps the companion.\n\nSee Traits & talents for spending points and Finding & recovery for state explanations. Summon limits, durations, costs, and supported actions come from the roster's configuration.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.bondedCompanions.visual=Active → Dismiss   Stored → Summon   Dead → Revive
tamework.ui.guide.topic.bondedCompanions.note=Bonded examples omit care meters. Summon availability still depends on capacity, cooldowns, and the roster's rules.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.travelFlight.title=Travel & flight
tamework.ui.guide.topic.travelFlight.body=HOME AND RECALL\nSet Home records a linked animal's home where supported. Return Home sends it there. Recall brings an eligible companion back under its travel rules. Off-screen or cross-world travel may retry or be restricted. Stored animals must leave their storage through the proper interaction.\n\nRIDING AND COMPANION MODES\nFollow the species' mount prompt, including any crouch or ownership requirement. Shoulder riding and grounded/airborne companion modes are separate features; their card icons show the current mode where supported.\n\nAVATAR FLIGHT\nAvatar flight uses a transformed player flight model and restores the source companion on dismount. Select Flightmaster's Talisman before activating this path. Read the flight HUD for your available launch, flap, boost, airbrake, and combat controls.\n\nWith the standard charged-launch setup, hold crouch on the ground and release after charging. Flaps and boosts use Vigour; efficient fast flight can restore it. Glide, dive, and climb manage speed and altitude. Ability bindings may be remapped. Cross-world transfer ends the flight session.\n\nSee Commands & groups for assignments and Finding & recovery for failed travel.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.travelFlight.visual=Home and recall • Riding • Avatar flight
tamework.ui.guide.topic.travelFlight.note=These are separate systems. Use the companion's mode controls for companion flight and the flight HUD for avatar abilities.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.worldUtilities.title=World utilities
tamework.ui.guide.topic.worldUtilities.body=SCARECROW PLACEMENT\nPlace a Tamework scarecrow on a solid surface. It faces the placer. Look at it and hold its collection prompt for one second to pick it up; if inventory is full, it stays placed.\n\nCOVERAGE\nAutomatic NPC spawn markers are paused within 32 blocks. Ordinary spawning is suppressed in native spawn chunks intersecting that area, so its horizontal edge can extend to a chunk boundary. Overlapping scarecrows remain active independently until collected.\n\nWHAT STILL EXISTS OR SPAWNS\nScarecrows do not remove existing creatures. Manually triggered spawn markers, spawner items, commands, breeding, recall, and other deliberate scripted spawns can still create or move animals.\n\nTROUGHS AND COOPS\nSupported troughs provide care resources and need refilling. Supported coops store and release animals. Their prompts and the companion's card explain the current state.\n\nSee Care & happiness for feeding and water, and Capture & coops for storage. Availability of these utilities depends on the installed content.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.worldUtilities.visual=Protected area ≠ empty area
tamework.ui.guide.topic.worldUtilities.note=The scarecrow limits automatic spawning. Animals already inside and deliberate arrivals such as breeding or recall remain possible.
